<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Jaora State</h1><p class="st-meta">1808 CE – 1948 CE (approx.) · capital Jaora</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Pathan Nawabi state in Malwa founded by a lieutenant of the freebooter Amir Khan, known for the Muharram shrine of Hussain Tekri.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdul-ghafur-khan">Abdul Ghafur Khan</a>, a Pathan cavalry commander and brother-in-law of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amir-khan-of-tonk">Amir Khan</a>, the founder of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tonk-state">Tonk State</a>, received Jaora and nearby lands from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holkars">Holkars</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/indore-state">Indore State</a> around 1808. The British confirmed his possession by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-mandsaur">Treaty of Mandsaur</a> in 1818.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Jaora was a 13-gun salute state of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa-agency">Malwa Agency</a>, with a Muslim ruling house over a mainly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> population growing opium and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a>. The Nawabs held <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/piploda-state">Piploda State</a> as a tributary. The shrine of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hussain-tekri">Hussain Tekri</a>, built by a Nawab in the 19th century, draws pilgrims during <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muharram">Muharram</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After accession to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> Jaora was merged into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/madhya-bharat">Madhya Bharat</a> in 1948 and joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhya-pradesh">Madhya Pradesh</a> in 1956; it is in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ratlam-state">Ratlam</a> district.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/jaora-state.jpg" alt="Jaora State" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Dr. Andreas Birken · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jaoraflag.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1808 CE – 1948 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>princely state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Jaora</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Abdul Ghafur Khan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism; Sunni Islam (ruling house)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Malvi</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Jaora</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1878&amp;focus=jaora-state" aria-label="Jaora State on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6683 1307 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Jaora State lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7003 1563L7364 1601M7003 1563L7364 1602M7003 1563L7064 1471"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7364 1601h0M7364 1602h0M7064 1471h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7003 1563h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7003 1563h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Jaora. <a href="/atlas?year=1878&amp;focus=jaora-state">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
